g the  scottish people to trust you  to lead an independent scotland so it  really  matters you're clearly an exceptional  communicator you're clearly somebody who  has strong approval ratings relative to  other politicians  but if you look at the facts record drug  deaths after 14 years of an s p  government still  inequalities in education although you  admit  that in in some areas there's been  improvement there are still big  inequalities when it comes to education  the highest rate of care home resident  deaths from covered in the uk  why should people trust you to lead an  independent scotland  sorry sophie the number of care home  deaths in scotland is far too high and  it shouldn't be a statistical  competition  but i really do have to challenge that  the number of  excess deaths in k homes in england has  been much higher  than in in scotland or higher than in  scotland  in scotland we have recorded more of  them as as  coveted death so i will challenge that  but that is not  saying that it is not the case that the  number of covered deaths and care homes  the number of covered deaths overall  uh hasn't been too high because it has  been like  people will decide whether they trust me  or not that's what elections  are all about i'll put my record in  front of the people of scotland i'll  point to all of the things  that we have done over the past term of  parliament we have  established a new social security system  in scotland we've established  a scottish child payment which is  lifting children out of poverty at the  level of child poverty too high  but lower in scotland than in any other  part of the uk  we have made progress in closing the  attainment gap we've led the country  through the covered pandemic i'll also  be candid  with people because i think it's better  to do that than the opposite  about where i think we haven't got  things right or where we have  more work to do and then people will  make a judgment about who they  trust most to lead the country through  covet  to get us into recovery and we put  before the scottish people a bold  ambitious  transformative program for recovery from  covid  and then after covert when the time is  right to decide  the future of scotland whether we should  leave big decisions in the hands of  politicians like boris johnson or  whether we should take those decisions  into our own hands as an independent  country so that we can  shape the long-term future of scotland  in the way we want it to be and and  people will make their judgments about  that they'll look at me my record what i  offer people  and they'll look at the opposition and  the competition as well and they'll  cast their verdict on may the 6th that's  democracy and i embrace it thoroughly  um alex salmond has launched a new party  to help build a supermajority for  scottish independence you must be very  grateful for his help  look he can speak for himself we need a  simple majority  in the scottish parliament to give us  the mandate for an independence  referendum and then of course we need a  majority of the scottish people to win  independence and you know when i listen  to people who talk about super  majorities they sound as if they think  we can just build those our way to  independence which is almost  contemptuous  uh towards those that we need to  persuade  the many many people have been acting  them i  i listen to the the rhetoric around  independence and it seems to skip  over they need to do the hard work of  persuading those who voted no in 2014  many of whom are now open-minded i know  many of these people but  want to be persuaded and want to be  treated with respect and want  us to make a convincing persuasive case  to them and  that's the hard work that i've been  doing polling for independence is at a  higher level now than it's ever been  but we've got to continue with that hard  work with that respectful  discourse and that's how we win  independence and you know i'm not  particularly interested in  an arm wrestling contest with other  people about who supports independence  the most  that's a conversation with ourselves i'm  interested in persuading those who are  open-minded but need still to be  persuaded so that we build a majority  for independence that then expresses  itself in a legitimate process  because that's how we win independence  and that's what i want to do  in the spirit of respectful discourse if  alba does win seats at the election  would you  work with alex salmond no i'm not going  to work with alex almond uh  firstly i have concerns about his  personal conduct that he hasn't  acknowledged or  apologized for as i've just said i i  don't  agree with the approach to independence  because i think it is  more or it is risking putting people off  rather than bringing people towards us  there's no shortcut to independence  we've got to do it through  winning a majority and allowing that  majority to express itself in a  legitimate process i also  don't know what albus stands for for me  independence is not an end in itself  it's  a means to a better scotland a fair uh  equal scotland where we value everybody  who lives here  for who they are and i don't know what  albus stands for but  from what i've seen so far i i probably  would have some concerns about that  okay um just finally we talked a lot  about lobbying  on the programme this morning the snp  minister fergus ewing had a dinner  with lex grunsel and sanjeev gupta in  2017. this is according to the sunday  mail  no officials were there no notes were  taken  if there's no record of what happened  how do we know it's above board  well it was recorded in the usual way in  the the register of  meetings look i understand the  temptation to draw an analogy between  the the scandal that we see developing  around david cameron  around the uk government and the  scottish government is dealing with gfg  the two things are very different we  work directly with gfg to firstly  uh save the dl steel works in 2015 there  was virtually nobody employed there  today it employs more than 100 people  and secondly with that company to save  the loca  aluminium smelter and you know there's  more than 100 jobs have been protected  there with more created as a result the  guarantee that was agreed  uh to support uh that takeover of the  smelter the committee of the scottish  parliament approved that  so it's not the the lobbying scandal  that we see elsewhere and i think people  should be careful  in trying to draw that analogy with all  the proper due diligence has been done  things like the the way in which green  cell has tried to to use the nhs  which david cameron allegedly lobbied  for that's not happened  in scotland and we've not had you know  some of the stuff around  covered loans so i can see the  temptation but the two things are not  the same
 

